# === DRAFT ONLY — NOT ADDED TO ANY CRONTAB — awaiting explicit approval ===
#
# v5 — supersedes v4 (this same file, prior version). Switches the `cd`
# target and the python/script invocation from the mutable runtime root
# (/root/AI-Stock-Radar-Control) to the immutable, provenance-verified
# release symlink (/root/AI-Stock-Radar-Control/deploy/current) --
# see scripts/deploy_control_arm_release.sh and
# scripts/verify_control_arm_deploy.sh, both real, tested in a local
# sandbox this same session (see that session's own report: git archive
# → SHA-256-verified tar transfer → atomic symlink swap → atomic
# DEPLOYED_VERSION.txt). Every cron invocation now runs whatever exact
# commit `deploy/current` currently points at -- explicit, auditable,
# and impossible to silently drift from what was approved, unlike
# invoking a live/mutable working-tree checkout directly. NOTHING here
# is live yet: no release has been deployed to the real server (this
# was deliberately NOT done this session -- sandbox-only, per explicit
# instruction), so `deploy/current` does not exist there yet either.
# `--env-file`, `--state-path`, `--decision-log-directory`,
# `--healthchecks-env-file`, and `AI_STOCK_RADAR_GUARD_DIR` all stay
# pinned to the STABLE runtime-root absolute paths, deliberately NOT
# release-relative -- persistent state/credentials/config must survive
# across releases, only the CODE (and the venv/guard/logs/data/live
# symlinks the deploy script creates inside each release, pointing right
# back at these same stable paths) is release-versioned.
#
# v4 (unchanged, still in effect): Healthchecks.io dead-man's-switch
# wiring on top of v3's fixes (all still in effect, unchanged — see the
# tail of this comment block for the full v2→v3 history). NOTHING
# healthchecks-related is live yet either: Levent has not created a
# Healthchecks.io account, so HEALTHCHECKS_LIVENESS_URL/
# HEALTHCHECKS_OPERATIONAL_URL are placeholders in
# /root/.config/ai-stock-radar-control/healthchecks.env (a file that
# does not exist on the server yet either). Every healthchecks-related
# piece below is written to degrade to a silent no-op if that file or
# those URLs are absent — see src/live/healthchecks_ping.py's own module
# docstring and run_control_arm_decision.py's `_load_healthchecks_config`
# for the in-process half of this same guarantee.
#
# TWO INDEPENDENT LIVENESS LAYERS, DELIBERATELY, NOT A DUPLICATION BUG:
# 1. The SHELL-level curl calls below (before/after the python command)
#    are the OUTER layer -- they fire even if Python itself fails to
#    start at all (a broken venv, a missing dependency, a syntax error
#    from a bad deploy) -- exactly the class of failure the in-process
#    code below can never detect, because it needs Python to already be
#    running correctly enough to read config and make an HTTP call.
# 2. run_control_arm_decision.py's own `main()`/`_execute()` ALSO pings
#    the SAME liveness check from inside Python (see that file's
#    "HEALTHCHECKS.IO DEAD-MAN'S-SWITCH" docstring section) -- this INNER
#    layer is what also drives the SEPARATE operational-state check
#    (STOP/FREEZE/lock-conflict/Telegram-delivery-failure/market-closed/
#    normal -- richer, domain-aware, only possible from inside Python).
# On a normal run both layers ping liveness successfully -- Healthchecks.io
# pings are idempotent check-ins, a harmless double ping, not an error.
# The redundancy is the entire point: the shell layer is what still tells
# you the cron job silently stopped running at all, even in the one case
# (Python won't start) the smarter in-process layer structurally cannot
# cover itself.
#
# Both curl blocks below are silently skipped if HEALTHCHECKS_LIVENESS_URL
# is unset/blank (sourcing a missing/incomplete healthchecks.env leaves it
# unset, `2>/dev/null` swallows the "file not found" from `.`, and the
# `[ -n "$HEALTHCHECKS_LIVENESS_URL" ]` guard skips every curl in that
# case) -- exactly the "silently disabled if not configured, never blocks
# the system" requirement, enforced at the shell layer too, not just in
# Python.
#
# `curl -fsS -m 10 --retry 3`: `-m 10` is a 10-second timeout per
# attempt, `--retry 3` retries transient failures up to 3 times (curl's
# own built-in retry, same 10s/3x envelope as the in-process
# `healthchecks_ping.py`), `-f` fails silently on an HTTP error status
# (so a bad ping never corrupts stdout), `-s`/`-S` stay quiet except for
# an actual error written to stderr (captured by this line's own `2>&1`
# redirect into the daily log, same as everything else on this line).
#
# PYTHON'S OWN EXIT CODE IS PRESERVED: `EXIT_CODE=$?` is captured
# IMMEDIATELY after the python command (before any curl call can
# overwrite `$?`), and the line ends with `exit $EXIT_CODE` -- cron sees
# the exact same exit status it would have seen with no healthchecks
# wrapping at all, so a real runner failure still shows up as a failed
# cron job in every other sense (mail-on-error, log monitoring, etc.),
# never masked by the wrapper.
#
# v2→v3 fixes (unchanged, still in effect):
#   1. --env-file /root/AI-Stock-Radar-Control/.env added to the command.
#      v2 relied on run_control_arm_decision.py's transitive imports
#      (src/data/alpaca_market_data.py, src/notify/telegram_notifier.py)
#      loading the repo-root .env normally at import time, with no
#      fail-closed check of that path at all -- the wrapper's own
#      fail-closed _load_env_file_fail_closed() function only ran when
#      --env-file was explicitly passed, which v2's cron entry never
#      did. Production and this session's own sandbox test were
#      therefore silently using two DIFFERENT credential-loading code
#      paths (implicit vs. explicit/fail-closed) -- the whole point of
#      building a fail-closed loader is defeated if production never
#      actually exercises it. Now both paths call the identical
#      function: Path(...).resolve(strict=True) (raises immediately if
#      the file is missing/unresolvable, never a silent fallback to
#      whatever else might already be in os.environ) + a check that
#      load_dotenv() actually reported loading something.
#   2. The BTC/universe leak check now runs in TWO layers, not one:
#      - NEW pre-flight check (_preflight_universe_check in
#        run_control_arm_decision.py), which reads the persisted state
#        file DIRECTLY and refuses to call run_daily_decision() AT ALL
#        -- zero API calls -- if it already contains any
#        position/pending-buy/pending-exit for BTC-USD or any ticker
#        outside the 44-ticker control universe. This is the layer that
#        actually matters for a STALE entry left behind by an earlier
#        run/bug/hand-edit -- v2's check ran only AFTER
#        run_daily_decision() had already fetched live data, written
#        new state, and (if enabled) could have submitted real orders,
#        so it could catch this run's OWN mistake but never a
#        pre-existing one.
#      - The v2 post-hoc audit check is KEPT, unchanged, as a second,
#        independent layer (if the pre-flight check somehow didn't fire,
#        this still would) -- not replaced, since a check that runs
#        after a real state-changing call is still worth having even
#        though it is provably weaker on its own.
#   3. --enable-crypto-orders' help text corrected: no longer claims
#      unconditional "no-op." BTC-USD can never generate a NEW entry
#      signal in this universe (RegimeAllowed force-set False), but if a
#      stale BTC-USD position/pending entry ever existed in state, this
#      flag's crypto order-submission logic (reused unmodified from
#      run_daily_decision.py) could still act on it -- item #2's new
#      pre-flight check is what actually prevents that scenario from
#      ever reaching this flag, not the flag's own semantics.
#
# (v2's fixes, unchanged, still in effect: real operational entry point
# -- scripts/run_control_arm_decision.py, not run_daily_decision.py
# directly; single-physical-line cron syntax; 22:02 UTC schedule, 47min
# after the live system's real 21:15 UTC run, not a multiple of 5 so it
# never lands in the same minute as the */5 crypto stop monitor.)
#
# DST — STILL AN OPEN RISK, STILL NOT RESOLVED: SSH to the production
# server is still blocked from this sandbox. A bare `H M * * 1-5` cron
# line runs at a fixed UTC instant regardless of US Eastern Time's DST
# transitions -- if the live system's 21:15 UTC is itself DST-adjusted
# by some external mechanism twice a year, this 22:02 UTC control-arm
# schedule needs the identical adjustment to keep the same 47-minute/
# non-multiple-of-5 relationship. ACTION ITEM, unchanged from v2/v3: once
# SSH access works, read the live crontab's actual text/mechanism and
# either mirror it here or confirm it is unnecessary.
#
# No --enable-equity-orders / --enable-crypto-orders flags: this stays
# a read-only signal-generation + paper-position-tracking run until the
# user explicitly approves enabling real paper order submission.
#
# Guard-file isolation (unchanged from v2/v3): AI_STOCK_RADAR_GUARD_DIR
# below points HIGH_WATER_MARK_PATH (src/live/position_state.py) at a
# control-arm-only directory -- without this it would default to
# $HOME/.ai_stock_radar_guard/ and silently collide with the live
# deployment's own guard file, since both run as root on the same
# server. This is the path already used in the deploy commands reported
# to the user (/root/AI-Stock-Radar-Control/.guard).

AI_STOCK_RADAR_GUARD_DIR=/root/AI-Stock-Radar-Control/.guard
02 22 * * 1-5 cd /root/AI-Stock-Radar-Control/deploy/current && set -a && . /root/.config/ai-stock-radar-control/healthchecks.env 2>/dev/null; set +a; if [ -n "$HEALTHCHECKS_LIVENESS_URL" ]; then curl -fsS -m 10 --retry 3 -o /dev/null -X POST "${HEALTHCHECKS_LIVENESS_URL}/start"; fi; /root/AI-Stock-Radar-Control/deploy/current/.venv/bin/python3 scripts/run_control_arm_decision.py --state-path /root/AI-Stock-Radar-Control/data/live/position_state.json --decision-log-directory /root/AI-Stock-Radar-Control/data/live/decisions --env-file /root/AI-Stock-Radar-Control/.env --healthchecks-env-file /root/.config/ai-stock-radar-control/healthchecks.env >> /root/AI-Stock-Radar-Control/logs/daily_decision.log 2>&1; EXIT_CODE=$?; if [ -n "$HEALTHCHECKS_LIVENESS_URL" ]; then if [ $EXIT_CODE -eq 0 ]; then curl -fsS -m 10 --retry 3 -o /dev/null -X POST "$HEALTHCHECKS_LIVENESS_URL"; else curl -fsS -m 10 --retry 3 -o /dev/null -X POST "${HEALTHCHECKS_LIVENESS_URL}/fail"; fi; fi; exit $EXIT_CODE
